--TEST--
a mis-cased file on disk is a miss for an autoload probe, not a diagnostic — `rule:programs/autoload`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `App\Mailer` resolves to `src/Mailer.nvs`, and what is on disk is
// `src/mailer.nvs`. The probe compares the directory entry's name exactly,
// so this is a miss on Windows and macOS too — a case-insensitive
// filesystem is not allowed to accept a program Linux refuses.
//
// The miss itself is silent: an unmatched name is an ordinary lookup
// failure, so what is reported is the undeclared class, from the site that
// named it.
require './src/Bootstrap.nvs';
echo App\Mailer::send(), "\n";
--FILE src/Bootstrap.nvs--
<?nvs
autoload 'App' from './';
--FILE src/mailer.nvs--
<?nvs
namespace App;

class Mailer
{
    public static function send(): string
    {
        return "sent";
    }
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0303]: `App\Mailer` is not declared
   --> case.nvs:11:6
   |
11 | echo App\Mailer::send(), "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^^ no matching declaration

error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
